  "answer_text": "There are **three ways** to change the color of a `<span>` on mouse over:\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 1: Using CSS `:hover` (Recommended)\n\nThis is the simplest and most common approach. You can create a hover effect using the `:hover` pseudo-class selector in CSS.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      span {\n        color: black;\n      }\n\n      span:hover {\n        color: red;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span>Hover over me!</span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 2: Using a CSS Class with `:hover`\n\nIf you want to target a **specific span** and not all spans, give it a class:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      .highlight {\n        color: black;\n      }\n\n      .highlight:hover {\n        color: blue;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span class=\"highlight\">Hover over me!</span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nYou can name your classes anything you want. Whatever name you choose, make sure the name used in your HTML matches that in your CSS rules.\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 3: Using JavaScript `onmouseover` / `onmouseout`\n\nYou can also use JavaScript events directly on the element:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <body>\n    <span\n      onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red'\"\n      onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black'\">\n      Hover over me!\n    </span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n> The `onmouseout` event is used here to revert the color back to its original state when the mouse leaves the element.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udd11 Summary\n\n| Method | Best For |\n|---|---|\n| `span:hover` (CSS) | Simple, clean, global styling |\n| `.class:hover` (CSS) | Targeting specific spans |\n| `onmouseover` (JS) | Dynamic or conditional color changes |\n\nThe **CSS `:hover` method** is generally preferred because it keeps styling separate from HTML and is easier to maintain.",
